  "notes": [
    "The source is a one-page semester-by-semester major map marked 'Proposed (120 credits)' with no year, so effectiveFromYear is 0.",
    "The map prints only course codes and per-semester credit totals: no titles, per-course credits, categories or prerequisites. All prereq values are null.",
    "Per-course credits were inferred from the semester totals: 3 credits each, except ENV107 (semester 1), CHE101 (semester 2) and BIO101/BIO103 (semester 5) at 4 credits (assumed 3 + 1 lab) so semesters 1, 2 and 5 total 10.",
    "The printed semester totals add up to 121, not 120: semester 9 prints 13 credits but has four courses (ENV313, ENV373, POL101/POL104, Major Elective). All four are kept at 3 credits (12), so the file sums to 120 and matches the header. If one of them is really 4 credits, the printed header 120 is wrong instead.",
    "English slots are shifted by waiver: semester 1 ENG102/ENG103, semester 2 ENG103/ENG105, semester 3 ENG105/ENG111 (a student with ENG102 waived takes ENG103, ENG105, ENG111). Modelled as three slots ENG102, ENG103, ENG105 with the printed alternatives.",
    "MAT112/MAT116 counts 3 credits here (semester 4 has four courses and totals 12), even though MAT116 is a 0-credit course in some NSU programs.",
    "ENV498 is printed '(Non-credit)', so nonCredit with 0 credits; semester 12 total 3 is the free elective. Its title is not printed (possibly an internship).",
    "Categories are not printed; grouped as General Education, Math & Science, Economics & Management, Major Core, Major Elective and Free Elective.",
    "Titles taken from other NSU maps in this set: ENV203, ENV373, ENV455, ENG111, ENG115, HIS101, HIS102, HIS205, POL101, POL104, MIS105, MAT116, MAT120, STA172, CHE101, BIO103. Titles from the NSU catalogue (less certain): MAT112 College Algebra, MGT210 Principles of Management, MGT314 Production Management, MGT489 Strategic Management. Titles not known and left as '(title not printed)': ENV205, ENV207, ENV208, ENV260, ECO260, ENV313, ENV315, ENV316, ENV354, ENV375, ENV409, ENV410, ENV498, BIO101."
